ntext:\nThunderbird is a free, open-source, cross-platform application for managing email and news feeds. It is a local (rather than a web-based) email application that is powerful yet easy-to-use.\n\nThunderbird is clean and elegant by default, but easily customizable to match your workflow and visual preferences. It is loaded with unique and powerful features.\n\nThunderbird is developed, tested, translated and supported by the folks at Mozilla Corporation and by a group of dedicated volunteers. Thunderbird gives you control and ownership over your email. There are lots of add-ons available for Thunderbird that enable you to extend and customize your email experience.\n\nThunderbird gives you IMAP\/POP support, a built-in RSS reader, support for HTML mail, powerful quick search, saved search folders, advanced message filtering, message grouping, labels, return receipts, smart address book LDAP address completion, import tools, and the ability to manage multiple e-mail and newsgroup accounts.\n\nThunderbird 155.0 changelog:\n\nWhat\u2019s New\n\n\n    S\/MIME popup shows signature, digest, encryption algorithms and key sizes\n    Default address book for manually added contacts configurable with mail.addr_book.add_item_default_uri\n    Enterprise policy DisableQRExport can disable QR export\n    OAuth add-ons can override built-in settings for selected domains and hostnames\n    Manual account config streamlined with Exchange, Graph, and custom OAuth support\n    Custom OAuth support for IMAP and SMTP servers\n    PKCE and external browser fields added to custom OAuth settings\n    Enterprise policy DisableExperimentalFeatures for disabling experimental features\n\n\nWhat\u2019s Fixed\n\n\n    Unread count dock badge disappeared on macOS restart when no mail window opened\n    IMAP tags did not sync automatically across clients\n    Embedded SVG images appeared broken when Display Attachments Inline was disabled\n    Holding arrow keys failed to smoothly navigate the folder pane\n    Mark and Tag menu bar items were disabled in search results\n    Quick Filter ignored address book display names shown in message lists and headers\n    Attachment filters could disappear or return no results in global search\n    Body search failed for quoted-printable messages ending on a soft line break\n    Failed encrypted send did not preserve draft\n    Failed account setup left an incoming server that blocked account recreation\n    Removing an account did not remove its OAuth token from the password manager\n    Account Hub prompted for calendar credentials immediately after successful login\n    Filters could copy empty messages to Local Folders during startup mail download\n    Closing message filters wrongly warned completed newsgroup filters were running\n    Autoscroll icon was missing directional arrows in scrollable messages\n    Apple Mail text attachments may not appear in MIME messages\n    Forwarded messages could display incorrect encoding\n    Thunderbird could crash due to improper database memory handling\n    Thunderbird could crash on shutdown while waiting for OAuth2 password entry\n    Thunderbird could crash while loading mail folders\n    Custom OAuth could not be disabled\n    Legacy IMAP tag changes were not sent to the server and reverted after refresh\n    Deleting an Exchange account retained the outgoing server settings\n    CalDAV calendars with offline support could stop fully synchronizing events\n    Offline cached ICS calendar changes were silently lost after reconnecting\n    Thunderbird upgrade disabled composition toolbar\n    Thunderbird upgrade disabled Settings menu for versions before macOS 13\n    Windows jump list menu no longer functioned correctly\n    Visual and UX improvements\n    Security fixes\n\n\nDownload: Thunderbird 155.0 for Windows (EN\/US) | 32-bit | ~80.0 MB (Open Source)\nDownload: Thunderbird 155.0 for Mac OS (EN\/US) | 151.0 MB\nDownload: Thunderbird 155.0 in other languages\nVie