[2026-09-02 15:34:02] [INFO] DEBUG: Moat Data Structure {"moat":{"_note":"Generated for COMPLEX_ENGINE classification. WACUP is a richly configurable media player with extensive plug-in support, skinning, and preferences.","content":[{"heading":"Enable 64-bit Build for Better Stability","recipe":"1. Download the WACUP x64 preview installer from the official site\n2. Install it into a separate folder from any existing 32-bit WACUP or Winamp install\n3. Launch the 64-bit build and import your existing settings via Preferences > General > Import Winamp\/WACUP settings\n4. Verify all installed plug-ins load under the 64-bit process before daily use","why":"Running the 64-bit build removes the 32-bit memory ceiling, reduces crashes when handling large playlists, and allows modern plug-ins that target 64-bit Windows to load without compatibility shims."},{"heading":"Configure Big Clock for Dual Display","recipe":"1. Locate your WACUP plug-ins folder (usually inside the install directory under Plugins)\n2. Copy gen_bigclock.dll and rename the copy to gen_bigclock2.dll\n3. Launch WACUP and open both plug-ins from the plug-in selection menu\n4. Set one instance to show elapsed time and the other to show remaining time using each window's display options","why":"This setup is popular among DJs, streamers, and radio hosts who need both elapsed and remaining time visible at a glance without switching windows."},{"heading":"Switch Playlist Path Mode for External Tools","recipe":"1. Open WACUP and go to Preferences > Playback > Advanced\n2. Locate the new playlist saving path mode option (relative or absolute)\n3. Choose Absolute if you sync playlists with external DJ software, media managers, or portable devices\n4. Save preferences and re-save an existing playlist to confirm the new path format","why":"Relative paths keep playlists portable across drives, but absolute paths are often required when third-party tools index or relocate the actual audio files."}]}}
